    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d773">

                <pb facs="0993" n="891" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg891"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/1541: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Ethiopia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Addis Ababa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-03T13:00:00+02:35">October 3, 1935—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>108. My 104, September 29, 9 p.m.<anchor n="97" xml:id="d773-f1"/> General
                    mobilization was proclaimed this morning and hostilities are reported to have
                    begun on the Eritrean border.</p>
                <p>I have insisted that three American women and six children of the Seventh Day
                    Adventist in Addis Ababa depart by next train.</p>
                <p>For all Americans remaining I have arranged with the British Legation to be
                    afforded such protection as may be possible. British Minister has kindly
                    consented to distribute gas masks to any Americans who desire them and to allow
                    them to set up tents in his compound in the event that police protection fails.
                    I have been repeatedly assured by the Emperor and local authorities that
                    everything possible will be done to protect American lives and property. Please
                    see paragraph 3, my 94, September 14, 10 p.m.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d773-f2"/>
                    and convey to Rome hope that American flags will be respected. The Department
                    might emphasize the fact that the American Legation is not in the same quarter
                    as the other Legations but at the opposite end of the city. I am still at the
                    Imperial Hotel as are also numerous other American citizens. The Italian
                    Minister who will probably leave in a few days is being informed.</p>
                <pb facs="0994" n="892" xml:id="pg892"/>
                <p>City is, so far, remarkably quiet but the atmosphere is naturally extremely
                    tense.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
